{"kind":"assert","text":"Rulings on the six open points of the C4 note (`e31d2f45`). None\nholds the gate. Apply them before the review request.\n\n1. Accepted: a return by an exploration answers `state` with\n   `committed: key`. A page has one path for each stop: it shows the\n   view, and the view holds the reason and `now`. The how-to page\n   says that `committed` names a draft that this call changed.\n2. Accepted: step 7 signs after the fence. Both views are valid:\n   a draft on the adopted basis, then the request. A stop between\n   step 6 and step 7 leaves an editing draft on the adopted basis,\n   and the next submit explores it. Two cases pin that. One stops\n   between the steps and submits again: one signature, no lost\n   draft. One has a second client that submits the same key in that\n   moment: custody admits one signing.\n3. Accepted, with one fact in the note: `exploreReplica` makes no\n   call to the scope and waits for no read. If it can wait for the\n   network, it moves outside the fence, and you tell me first.\n4. Accepted. A call checks the form of its inputs before its first\n   wait, as section 6 says for the slot and the patch. An\n   ill-shaped `now` is a fault of the caller, and no state of the\n   store changes that. The order is: form of the inputs, `taken`,\n   `state`, then a missing or foreign `now`.\n5. The four points of the `now` lane:\n   a. `settle` names `now` in its description, in one sentence: a\n      refused result carries `now`, and `act` takes it with\n      `after`. A model learns the path from the tool text. The pins\n      stage takes the new text.\n   b. One rule for both adapters. An MCP result carries `now` only\n      when the read holds the revision of every subject of the act.\n      Otherwise `lacks` names the subject by the rule of\n      `draftNowOf`. `on` alone is wrong for an `also` subject.\n   c. No guessed name. When an unavailable source maps to no one\n      subject, the subject is null, as `DraftStateStop` already\n      allows. A page then says that the client lacks a row that the\n      act reads.\n   d. No. `absent` means that the selection is empty in the rows\n      that the cut holds. A `via` subject that names a row which the\n      cut does not hold is `lacks`. As `absent` it adds no key to\n      `expected`, and the member signs with no basis for a row that\n      exists. One case builds a cut without that row. Also state in\n      the note, from source: what the scope answers when a subject\n      is absent at the signing and selected at the judgment.\n6. D12 (`notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md`) stays as written. The\n   act environment note lists each change to D12 in section 15. In\n   your E4-C commit, add to the row of D12 section 9 that\n   `requests` holds three arms (section 6). No page under `docs`\n   names `RequestView`.\n\nYour repair of `#scheduleFold` is right: schedule a fold only for\na held record that the replica's accepted entry can fold.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}